<div class="main-title">Likutay Halachos &mdash; Choshen Mishpat II</div>
<div class="sub-title">Hilchos Umnin [Laws of Craftsmen]<br>Halacha 4</div>

<div class="halacha-title">Umnin &mdash; Halacha 4: Part D (&sect;&sect;18&ndash;23)</div>

<div class="section-marker">&sect;18 [marked as ois yud-tes in text]</div>

<p>And all of this we need during these days, before <span class="key-term">Sh&rsquo;mini Atzeres</span>, which is Simchas Torah &mdash; when we conclude and begin the Torah. That is, because now are the days when we need to complete the Torah that we have been reading throughout the entire year, and to return to the beginning. Therefore we engage in all the tikunim mentioned above &mdash; through which we give the letters of the Torah a good configuration, in the aspect of the potion of life [sam hachayim] &mdash; so that all the Torah that we engaged in throughout the entire year should be configured and combined for good.</p>

<p>And likewise, we should merit to begin the Torah anew, to read it through good vowel-points, so that the Torah should from now on be configured and combined always only for good and for life and for peace. Through which the entire world will be repaired. For the entire world and its fullness is directed and sustained through the Torah, and according to the configuration with which we configure and combine the letters of the Torah through the yearnings &mdash; so is the world directed, for good or, G&ndash;d forbid, the reverse.</p>

<p>Therefore Hashem, blessed be He, had mercy upon us in His great mercy, and granted us &mdash; through His holy sages who arranged for us to conclude and begin the Torah on the day of the completion of the tikun of Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim, which is Sh&rsquo;mini Atzeres and Simchas Torah &mdash; so that through the holy breaths of Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim, which are holy souls from which are made holy vowel-points, the entire Torah should be configured and combined for good. Both all that we read throughout the entire year, which we are now concluding, and that which we are going to read in the entire coming year, which we are now beginning &mdash; everything should be configured and combined for good and for life, through the holy breaths of these awesome days, during which Israel overpowers with abundant holy breaths, etc., as above.</p>

<p>And therefore it is called Sh&rsquo;mini <span class="key-term">Atzeres</span> &mdash; a language of gathering and collecting [k&rsquo;nisah va&rsquo;asifah]. For then we gather and collect all the letters of the Torah to configure them for good, through the good souls and vowel-points that were made on Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim, as above. For before good vowel-points are drawn, the letters have no connection or joining. Only through good vowel-points &mdash; through this they pair and join and are gathered to perform good actions. Therefore it is called Atzeres, a language of gathering and collecting.</p>

<div class="section-marker">&sect;19 [marked as ois chaf in text]</div>

<p>For the four species are the aspect of the four letters of the Name [Hashem] &mdash; which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta&rsquo;amim, n&rsquo;kudos, tagin, osiyos</span> [cantillation notes, vowel-points, crowns, letters] &mdash; through which one draws good vowel-points to the letters, through the holy breaths, as above. For the essential tikun is drawn from the root of the letters and the vowel-points, which are the four letters of the Name &mdash; where is the root of all the ta&rsquo;amim, n&rsquo;kudos, tagin, and osiyos. And through the holy four species, which are grasped there, we draw upon ourselves the tikun from there during these days, through the power of the holy breaths, from which are made holy vowel-points to give the letters of the Torah a good configuration, as above.</p>

<p>For when the letters pair and join together through good yearnings, from which are made good vowel-points for the letters, as above &mdash; then all the four aspects of ta&rsquo;amim, n&rsquo;kudos, tagin, and osiyos are included together. And this is the aspect of the four species &mdash; through which is the completeness of the tikun of all the worlds, as is known, and as above.</p>

<div class="section-marker">&sect;20 [marked as ois chaf-alef in text]</div>

<p>And behold, all our words here are built upon the Torah teaching &ldquo;Is lan beira b&rsquo;dabra&rdquo; [&ldquo;We have a well in the field&rdquo;] in siman 31. And it is explained there at the end that this Torah teaching was said on the verse: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Vayaseiv Elokim&rdquo;</span> [&ldquo;And G&ndash;d led the people around&rdquo;] &mdash; &ldquo;hasibah is nothing but a feast [s&rsquo;udah],&rdquo; etc. See there. However, we did not merit to receive from him the complete explanation of the verse according to this Torah teaching.</p>

<p>And what appears to my humble understanding regarding this is: &ldquo;Vayaseiv Elokim&rdquo; is the aspect of a feast, as above &mdash; that is, the aspect of the eating and the sustenance that one must bring from outside to inside, as above. And this is accomplished through: <span class="verse">&ldquo;the way of the wilderness, the Yam Suf&rdquo;</span> [derech hamidbar Yam Suf] &mdash; <span class="verse">&ldquo;and armed&rdquo;</span> [vachamushim], etc.</p>

<p>&ldquo;The wilderness&rdquo; [hamidbar] &mdash; this is the aspect of emunah, which depends on speech [dibbur]. For one must speak the holy emunah with a full mouth, as Rabbainu of blessed memory wrote: &ldquo;The emunah depends on the mouth of a person,&rdquo; etc. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;Yam Suf&rdquo; [the Sea of Reeds/End]. For emunah, the aspect of speech, the aspect of malchus, is the aspect of a sea [yam] into which all the streams flow, as is known. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;suf&rdquo; [end] &mdash; for it is the aspect of the end and the completeness of all the attributes and of the entire creation, as is brought there in the Torah teaching mentioned above, in the aspect of: &ldquo;Chavakuk came and stood them all upon emunah,&rdquo; etc.</p>

<p>&ldquo;And armed&rdquo; [vachamushim], etc. &mdash; and Rashi explained: &ldquo;with weapons&rdquo; [m&rsquo;zuyanim]. This is the aspect of tikun habris, which is the essential weapon &mdash; in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Gird your sword upon your thigh, O mighty one&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Tehillim 45:4)</span>, etc. That is, through tikun habris, through which one merits emunah, the aspect of Shabbos &mdash; through this one merits to bring the sustenance inside, to Israel. And this is the aspect of: &ldquo;Vayaseiv Elokim es ha&rsquo;am,&rdquo; as above.</p>

<p>Also, &ldquo;hamidbar Yam Suf&rdquo; hints at the good souls that are made from good yearnings, whose essential emergence into actuality is through speech [dibbur], as is explained there. And this is also the aspect of &ldquo;Yam Suf&rdquo; &mdash; for they are the aspect of the end [sof] and the completeness of all things. For they give the letters of the Torah a good configuration, through which all things are completed and finished for good.</p>

<p>And in truth it is all one. For the tikun of emunah and the tikun of these good vowel-points that are made from the souls and good yearnings &mdash; it is all one. For the more a person has emunah, he certainly yearns and longs all the more with good yearnings. For he knows that there is no purpose in the world except to do His will, blessed be He &mdash; only that he has many obstacles and hindrances to this. Therefore he certainly yearns and desires and longs at every moment: &ldquo;When will I merit to reach this, to be truly as He wills, blessed be He?&rdquo;</p>

<p>And for this reason, emunah is the completeness of all things. And the entire Torah and all the blessings and all the abundance &mdash; nothing is completed except through emunah, as is explained there at the beginning of the Torah teaching mentioned above. For through emunah, which is drawn through the power of the tzaddik, through tikun habris &mdash; through this, many souls are made through good yearnings, which are the aspect of good vowel-points that give the letters of the Torah a good configuration, that they should make and accomplish good actions in the world, as above. And this is the essential tikun and completeness of the Torah and of all things in the world that are directed and sustained through it.</p>

<p>It emerges that the tikun of good yearnings and the tikun of emunah &mdash; it is all one. For they complete and repair everything through giving the letters of the Torah a good configuration, as above. And with this, the Torah teaching mentioned above is well connected and joined &mdash; see there.</p>

<p>And this is why <span class="verse">&ldquo;And they traveled from Sukkos&rdquo;</span> [vayis&rsquo;u miSukkos] <span class="inline-source">(Shemos 13:20)</span> is placed right after the verse &ldquo;Vayaseiv Elokim.&rdquo; For &ldquo;vayis&rsquo;u miSukkos&rdquo; hints at the mitzvah of the holy sukkah, which Israel merited immediately upon their departure from Egypt &mdash; when He surrounded and encompassed them with Clouds of Glory, which are the aspect of the sukkah, etc. As it is written there immediately in the parashah: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And Hashem went before them by day in a pillar of cloud,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Shemos 13:21)</span>. And all of this is placed next to &ldquo;Vayaseiv Elokim,&rdquo; which is the aspect of eating and sustenance &mdash; bringing the sustenance from outside to inside. For this one merits through the mitzvah of sukkah &mdash; through which one brings the sustenance from outside to inside, as above.</p>

<div class="divider">&mdash; Let us return to the matter of the guardians &mdash;</div>

<div class="section-marker">&sect;22</div>

<p>And this is the aspect of the law of the <span class="key-term">socheir</span> [renter/hirer] &mdash; as is written there, that he is liable for theft and loss and exempt from unavoidable circumstances. For the essential guarding from the damaging forces of the world [mazikay alma] is through the true tzaddik, who is the aspect of the &ldquo;master of the soul&rdquo; [ba&rsquo;al nefesh], from whom are drawn all the holy yearnings, which are the aspect of good breaths and souls that give the letters of the Torah a good configuration.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Shir Shel P&rsquo;ga&rsquo;im</span> [the Song Against Harmful Spirits] that is said there <span class="inline-source">(Tehillim 91)</span>: <span class="verse">&ldquo;A thousand shall fall at your side, etc.; it shall not approach you. No evil shall befall you, etc. Upon the lion and the viper you shall tread; you shall trample the young lion and the serpent. For he desires Me, and I will deliver him&rdquo;</span> [ki vi chashak va&rsquo;afal&rsquo;taihu]. &ldquo;For he desires Me&rdquo; specifically. For the psalm explains through what one is saved from all the harmful forces and merits to tread upon all the k&rsquo;lipos and the damaging forces of the world, in the aspect of: &ldquo;upon the lion and the viper you shall tread.&rdquo; All this one merits because of: &ldquo;For he desires Me [ki vi chashak],&rdquo; etc.</p>

<p>For through the greatness of the desire and the holy yearnings with which he desires Him, blessed be He &mdash; through this: &ldquo;I will deliver him.&rdquo; For all the damaging forces of the world &mdash; their essential nourishment is from evil yearnings and evil breaths, which are evil souls. And they themselves are the damaging forces, G&ndash;d protect us. For they cause damage in the physical and the spiritual realms, in the aspect of: &ldquo;There is a hevel that is done.&rdquo; For these breaths are the aspect of souls that go and arouse toward evil, G&ndash;d forbid &mdash; from which come all the damages, G&ndash;d protect us. And therefore the essential subduing and nullifying of them is through the tzadikim and the k&rsquo;shairim [upright people] who have good yearnings, which are good souls that arouse the world toward good &mdash; and they subdue and nullify all the damaging forces of the world, in the aspect of: &ldquo;Upon the lion and the viper you shall tread,&rdquo; etc., &ldquo;for he desires Me, and I will deliver him,&rdquo; as above.</p>

<div class="section-marker">&sect;23</div>

<p>And therefore the tzaddik is called a <span class="key-term">tzofeh</span> [watchman] and a shomer [guardian], as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;I have appointed you as a watchman&rdquo;</span> [tzofeh n&rsquo;satticha] <span class="inline-source">(Yechezkel 3:17)</span>. And Rashi explained: a guardian [shomer]. For the essential guarding is through him &mdash; because through him one merits the holy yearnings, through which is the essential guarding, as above.</p>

<p>And if everyone would merit this in completeness &mdash; the aspect of the tzaddik, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">bris ila&rsquo;ah</span> [the upper covenant], the aspect of guarding the bris in completeness &mdash; they certainly would not need any guarding in the physical realm. For all the damaging forces of the world would be nullified through the good yearnings and good souls that are drawn through the tzaddik, as above.</p>

<p>However, the essential reason that guarding is also needed in the physical realm &mdash; this is drawn from the aspect of the <span class="key-term">thirty-nine melachos</span> [categories of labor] that are drawn from the aspect of <span class="key-term">bris tata&rsquo;ah</span> [the lower covenant], which is the aspect of na&rsquo;ar [a youth/servant], the aspect of an eved [servant]. From which come all the labors and the toils of the affairs of this world &mdash; which are all included in the thirty-nine melachos. And from there is drawn the need for guarding also in the physical realm.</p>

<p>For the essential thirty-nine melachos are drawn from the sin of Adam HaRishon, who ate from the <span class="key-term">Aitz HaDa&rsquo;as Tov vaRa</span> [the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil]. For then it was decreed: <span class="verse">&ldquo;By the sweat of your brow shall you eat bread,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Beraishis 3:19)</span> &mdash; which are the thirty-nine melachos, as is known. And the essential blemish of the Aitz HaDa&rsquo;as Tov vaRa was the aspect of the blemish of those who entered the Pardais and did not depart in peace like Rabbi Akiva, as is understood in the Tikunim [Tikunay Zohar].</p>

<p>And the essential blemish of those ones was because they did not give the letters of the Torah a good configuration through good yearnings, and they did not include the aspect of the lamdan [the scholar] and the tzaddik together. Rather, they made a separation between the aspect of lamdan and the aspect of tzaddik &mdash; and this is the aspect of &ldquo;chopping the plantings&rdquo; [m&rsquo;katzeitz ban&rsquo;ti&rsquo;os], as is well understood in the Torah teaching mentioned above &mdash; see there well.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the blemish of the scholars who dispute the tzadikim, who say that the essential thing is only the study itself, and there is no need to draw close to tzadikim to repair one&rsquo;s deeds at all. And in the end, neither this nor that remained in their hands. For afterward they cast even the study of Torah behind their backs &mdash; as is common, due to our many sins, with many people who in the days of their youth engaged in Torah somewhat, and afterward cast off the yoke of Torah entirely, and also corrupted their deeds more than the unlearned people. And all this is because they did not pay attention to repairing their deeds at all, and did not endeavor to draw close to true tzadikim. And they said that the essential thing is the study. And in the end, neither this nor that was maintained in their hands.</p>

<p>For in truth, the essential tikun of the study of Torah is through the aspect of the tzaddik. And one who separates the aspect of lamdan from the aspect of tzaddik &mdash; he is the aspect of Acher who chopped the plantings. For the purpose of Torah is teshuvah and good deeds, as our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;One should not read and study and then kick against his father and his teacher,&rdquo; etc. For the aspect of the lamdan is in the aspect of bris tata&rsquo;ah, the aspect of a servant &mdash; which is beneath bris ila&rsquo;ah, which is the aspect of a free man [ben chorin], the aspect of Avraham.</p>

<p>And when the lamdan is subordinate to the tzaddik, then he merits to give the letters of the Torah a good configuration, through the power of the tzaddik who is the teacher who resembles an angel of Hashem Tz&rsquo;vakos &mdash; before whom the letters of the Torah seek to be configured, etc., as is explained there. But when he is not subordinate to him &mdash; all the more so when he, G&ndash;d forbid, disputes him &mdash; then: &ldquo;he did not merit, and it became a potion of death for him.&rdquo; For he gives the letters of the Torah the reverse configuration, G&ndash;d forbid.</p>

<p>The general principle is that the essential tikun of all the worlds is through the true tzadikim, who are at the ultimate level of guarding the bris in truth and in completeness. For he specifically merits to give the letters of the Torah a good configuration, that they should make and accomplish good actions in the world, to repair everything. And even one who does not merit this, to reach the aspect of the tzaddik in completeness &mdash; even so, if he humbles himself at least beneath the tzaddik as a servant beneath his master, then all his Torah and his study and all his deeds receive tikun through the tzaddik, who gives everything a good configuration. And then it is said of him also: &ldquo;He merited, and it became a potion of life for him&rdquo; &mdash; through the power of the tzaddik.</p>

<p>But conversely, if he does not humble himself, G&ndash;d forbid, beneath the tzaddik &mdash; all the more so when he disputes him, G&ndash;d forbid &mdash; then it is said of him: &ldquo;He did not merit,&rdquo; etc. For he separates the aspect of lamdan from the aspect of tzaddik &mdash; and this is the aspect of &ldquo;chopping the plantings,&rdquo; from which is the essential side of death. For this is the aspect of the blemish of the Aitz HaDa&rsquo;as Tov vaRa, as above &mdash; when it was decreed: <span class="verse">&ldquo;For on the day you eat of it,&rdquo;</span> etc., as above.</p>

